WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.220
It might be the A-list stars donning the capes
and masks, but the real heroes of today's

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.790
CGI-heavy comic book movies are the special
effects artists — the men and women who

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.320
really make it all possible.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:15.740
Of course, if you're not convinced that's
the case, just try watching those movies before

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:16.980
the effects were added in.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.900
2014 was a big year for Marvel movies — beyond
the obvious successes of the MCU, Fox also

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.840
released its eagerly awaited X-Men sequel
Days of Future Past.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.980
As Marvel prepared to introduce Aaron Taylor-Johnson
as the MCU's Quicksilver in Avengers: Age

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:38.860
of Ultron, Fox exercised its right to use
the character by casting Evan Peters as the

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:42.240
lightning-fast mutant for the X-Men prequel/sequel
combo.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:47.560
The film earned rave reviews, and Peters'
Quicksilver was one of its best parts, with

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.040
one scene in particular making the headlines.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:56.300
Days of Future Past's now-iconic super slow-mo
kitchen scene may have looked amazing on film,

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.440
but it wasn't at all easy to achieve.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:04.680
The sequence had to be shot at a mind-blowing
3,600 frames per second — compare that to

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.060
the standard 24 for most films.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:10.760
A frame rate that high requires huge amounts
of light to work.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.359
In an interview with io9, Peter said:

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.120
"It was incredibly bright.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.539
A lot of the crew and Bryan [Singer] got to
wear sunglasses.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:23.780
And the actors had to keep their eyes open
for a long, long time, with that bright light

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.060
just blaring at you.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.320
It's like the sun.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:27.880
It's brighter than the sun.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:29.280
It's right there in your eyes.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.480
But the end result is so worth it.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:32.880
You just power through it."

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:39.040
Unlike its predecessor, 2016's X-Men Apocalypse
didn't go down too well with the critics.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:44.800
Still, it had a few redeeming features, chief
among which was — yeah, you guessed it — Quicksilver's

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.100
moment in the spotlight.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.240
Why mess with perfection, right?

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.759
Although technological advances made between
Days of Future Past and Apocalypse made things

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:58.000
easier for the effects team, director Bryan
Singer still chose to approach Quicksilver's

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.160
second major set piece as practically as possible.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:06.619
This super slow-mo evacuation scene may have
upped the ante from the kitchen scene in Days

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:11.310
of Future Past, but Singer told Den of Geek
that you'd be surprised just how much of it

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:11.900
was real.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:12.680
He explained:

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.420
"We did use certain visual effects, certain
digital effects and explosive algorithms,

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:22.989
but we also took multiple phantom 3D cameras
and ran them in protective cases through physical

00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:23.920
explosions."

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.560
Just because the CGI team had it easy, however,
doesn't mean everyone did.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.420
"We blew up our sets.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.820
We waited until we were done with them, and
then blew them up.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:38.980
We flew the cameras through at 80mph rolling
at 3000 frames per second."

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.240
All in all, it took six weeks to make what
amounted to two minutes of film.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:47.260
Considering how well that scene went down
with fans, however, there's a good chance

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.360
the crew decided it was more than worth the
effort.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:56.640
The fifth MCU entry marked Chris Evans' debut
as Steve Rogers, a scrawny New Yorker who

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.680
becomes an all-American super soldier after
agreeing to take part in a military experiment.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.420
Evans got jacked for the role, though his
bulky frame meant that filming the scenes

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.700
that take place before he transforms into
Captain America was very tricky.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.760
The visual effects department shrunk Evans'
actual body for some shots, but they also

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.579
used a body double: actor Leander Deeny.

00:03:18.579 --> 00:03:22.319
"The goal was to make the audience fall in
love with the character before he gets any

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.480
muscles, before he puts on any costumes, before
he holds the shield."

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.900
Visual effects put Evans' face on Leander's
body.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:33.420
And although his double was diligent about
mimicking Evans' body language as best he

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.939
could, the effects company still had their
work cut out, comparing it to, quote, "taking

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:41.420
the head of a rhinoceros and putting it on
the body of a gazelle."

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:48.020
Logan was a milestone movie in Fox's long-running
X-Men franchise, not only because it was Hugh

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:52.650
Jackman's final bow as Wolverine, but also
because it was the first time fans got to

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:55.920
see the clawed mutant in all his R-rated glory.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.780
The levels of violence on display during Logan
were like nothing before seen in the X-Men

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:04.380
universe, and this meant the VFX team had
their work cut out for them.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:09.520
But Rising Sun Pictures' VFX supervisor Dennis
Jones relished the challenge.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.340
He told Art of VFX:

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:17.300
"Logan is more brutal, visceral, and has direct
consequences for the characters involved.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:22.330
The R rating that was confirmed from the start
introduced another dynamic to play with, albeit

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:23.580
in a restrained fashion.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.800
In The Wolverine, some of our shots had to
be amended to remove blood and claw penetration,

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.980
so it was great to be able to [set] the character
free from these constraints."

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:37.110
But that gore went far beyond just computer
graphics.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.240
Makeup designer Joel Harlow also told The
Verge that:

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.650
"The level of physical trauma in this film
is something I haven't had to contend with

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:47.080
since the early years when I was smearing
blood around."

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:53.670
Guardians of the Galaxy: Vol 2's visual effects
supervisor Chris Townsend wasn't part of the

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:57.780
team that worked on the first Guardians movie,
but he had previously worked with Marvel on

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:02.750
Captain America: The First Avenger and Iron
Man 3, receiving an Oscar nomination for the

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:03.240
latter.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.100
When offered the opportunity to oversee the
Guardians sequel, Townsend jumped at the chance

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.360
to return to the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:11.610
He told Rotoscopers:

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.720
"I'm a huge fan of the films.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:18.040
I think from a creative and visual effects
point of view, they're some of the funnest

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:19.040
stuff out there."

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:24.860
For the film's epic third act, in which the
Guardians face Ego inside his own planet,

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:29.910
Townsend accepted a bid from Weta Digital,
who picked up the action right after Ego reveals

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.060
to Quill that he gave his mother cancer.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.120
Weta's Guy Williams told Art of VFX:

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.320
"From that point on, it is mostly our work.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:43.700
We go all the way until Yondu and Quill rise
above the planet as it is destroyed behind

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:44.200
them."

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:49.820
The New Zealand-based visual effects house
had up to 490 staffers working on Guardians

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:55.010
of the Galaxy: Vol 2 at one stage, according
to Williams, who admitted to losing plenty

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:57.910
of sleep over the highly complicated nature
of the project.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.820
Let's face it, if you're the King of Wakanda,
you've got to ride in style.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:07.520
And with the world's largest supply of the
miracle metal vibranium at your disposal,

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:11.920
a Rolls Royce or an Air Force One-style Boeing
747 simply won't do.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.440
Luckily, those Wakandan engineers and scientists
clearly knew what they were doing when they

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.540
designed Black Panther's jet.

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:24.040
When King T'Challa arrived in Wakanda from
his journeys abroad, he did so on a badass,

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:27.400
super-sleek stealth plane that looks like
it came right out of Area 51.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:30.500
You wouldn't know that if you were just watching
the raw dailies, though.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:35.380
In the original footage, there was no jet,
no landing platform — heck, there wasn't

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:36.380
even any Wakanda.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:41.040
There were just a handful of actors on set
and a large blue screen in the background.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.740
It's a little jarring to see that the Mad
Titan, the destroyer of civilizations, the

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:51.620
wicked warlord Thanos, is actually… well,
it's Josh Brolin wearing what looks like an

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.520
overpriced, skin-tight biker's outfit.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:58.460
While Brolin is probably thankful he didn't
have to wear space armor and paint his skin

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:02.591
purple for the role, he was still very much
present during the filming of Infinity War

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.900
and Endgame thanks to the wonders of motion
capture technology.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:10.700
Brolin had never done mo-cap before, but he
saw the light after seeing what his future

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:16.210
Avengers castmate Benedict Cumberbatch was
able to do as Smaug in The Hobbit: The Desolation

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:16.940
of Smaug.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:23.880
"I kill wherever I breathe and none shall stop me."

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:29.740
Indeed, Brolin's one-on-one interactions with
his castmates added an extra layer of creepy

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:34.040
credibility that made the Mad Titan that much
scarier in the final movies.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.360
One of the most underrated aspects of Avengers:
Infinity War was Peter Dinklage's performance

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.430
as Eitri, the King of the Dwarves.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:47.200
Here, the actor — who only stands about
4'4" — was playing a 20-foot giant.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.980
The Lord of the Rings films famously used
forced perspective to make people look like

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:57.830
Hobbit-sized, but this film used a different
approach, instead having Dinklage play opposite

00:07:57.830 --> 00:08:00.920
mini-cutouts of Thor, Groot and Rocket Raccoon.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:06.400
This makes Dinklage's scene-stealing performance
that much more impressive, as he was basically

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:07.880
performing with dolls.

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:12.940
Chris Hemsworth, who also had to act opposite
nothing, would shoot his scenes on a set,

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:15.000
while Dinklage performed against a blue-screen.

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:18.020
It's not always easy, this acting malarkey.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:24.960
Infinity War's climactic Battle of Wakanda
was filmed on an 8,000-acre farm called Chattahoochee

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.100
Hills Eventing, about 40 minutes outside Atlanta.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:35.320
Instead of thousands of extras, only 70 performers
were used, but they became more than 500 Wakandans

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:40.450
digitally, and proceeded to do battle with
Thanos' army of 10,000 alien monsters known

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:43.360
as Outriders — which were also digitally
produced.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:48.240
The art department even added indigenous African
trees and created an artificial river for

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.720
the scene, with a massive pump that was gushing
out up to 30,000 gallons per minute.

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:57.340
That's a lot of effort for something that's
barely even visible in the finished movie.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:03.200
Thor's arrival at the Battle of Wakanda may
very well be the most epic superhero entrance

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:03.700
ever.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:08.900
But when you see what it looked like before
the CGI was added… okay, it's still pretty

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:11.700
epic, but it's not quite the same as the finished
product.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:17.760
For this scene, Chris Hemsworth donned his
full Thor attire and wielded a massive axe

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.020
that just need a little CG love to become
the mighty Stormbreaker.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:28.340
Of course, what he didn't have was a smack-talking,
gun-toting raccoon on his shoulder, an angsty

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:34.320
teenage tree, hundreds of Wakandan warriors,
or an invading army of 10,000 alien monsters.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.560
So yeah, most of the stuff in this scene was
added after the fact.

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:41.700
All the more reason to be impressed with Chris
Hemsworth's performance, considering he was

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:45.340
basically acting by himself in a $10,000 Halloween
costume.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:51.080
Following Hulk's vicious beatdown at the hands
of Thanos in Avengers: Infinity War, Marvel

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:55.510
fans were on the edges of their seats waiting
to find out what the future held for everyone's

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:57.120
favorite angry green giant.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:02.040
None but the most die-hard comic book fans
could have guessed it would be Professor Hulk.

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:07.980
According to Animation Boss, it was Framestore
who breathed life into Endgame's Bruce Banner/Hulk

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:08.480
hybrid.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:13.260
The process started by taking source footage
of Mark Ruffalo and matching it to animation.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:19.240
This animation was then used for 60 shots,
with head cam footage used for reference.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:22.780
According to Animation Supervisor Max Solomon:

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:26.520
"It was absolutely about channeling Bruce
Banner.

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:28.360
His face is very expressive.

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:33.680
Every nuance, every little twitch and eye
dart, slight curl of the lip and muscle tension

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:35.100
needed to be translated."

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.440
VFX Supervisor Stuart Penn explained:

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:43.880
"There was a lot of work involved as their
face shapes are so different — Hulk is massive,

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:48.120
his mouth proportionally bigger, his eyes
are sunken with deeper sockets.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.880
Tiny changes to his face had a huge effect
on the performance."

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:58.300
Having wiped out half of all life in Infinity
War, a past version of Thanos returns to Earth

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:03.240
in Endgame's final act, in a desperate attempt
to regain the six infinity stones from the

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:03.740
Avengers.

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:08.620
Unfortunately for him, this is the first time
this version of the Mad Titan has gone up

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:10.040
against against Captain Marvel.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:15.260
Fans had been eagerly anticipating Captain
Marvel's bout against Thanos ever since she

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:18.840
was teased in the post-credits scene to Avengers:
Infinity War.

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:24.840
After seeing what she was capable during both
her own movie and her handful of earlier scenes

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:29.940
in Endgame, it became clear that the Mad Titan
had more than met his match in Danvers.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:34.800
But bringing this fight to life on set appears
to have been considerably less epic than how

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:36.620
it wound up being on the big screen.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:39.400
But hey, that's the magic of the movies for
you.

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:45.140
Tony Stark's last stand against Thanos has
to be one of the MCU's greatest ever scenes.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:54.320
"And I am Iron Man."

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:59.960
That final line was a call-back to one first
uttered 11 years prior to Endgame in Iron

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:03.860
Man, the film that launched the biggest franchise
in history — and there couldn't have been

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:05.960
a better line for Tony Stark to go out on.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:09.040
Strangely enough, however, it almost didn't
happen.

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:13.980
During a Q&amp;A that took place in the wake of
the film's release, the Russo brothers explained:

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:16.940
"Tony used to not say anything in that moment.

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:20.700
And we were in the editing room going, 'He
has to say something.

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:24.420
This is a character who has lived and died
by quips.'

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:29.040
[...] And our editor Jeff Ford, who's been
with us all four movies and is an amazing

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:34.520
storyteller, said, 'Why don't we just go full
circle with it and say 'I am Iron Man.”

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.040
And we're like, 'Get the cameras!

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:37.660
We have to shoot this tomorrow.'"

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:42.800
The scene turned out the be the last one filmed
during Endgame's reshoots, and the behind-the-scenes

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:47.760
reality of this iconic moment was thankfully
captured by Downey's assistant Jimmy Rich.

